          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"category\":\"digitizer_configuration_or_readout_anomaly\",\"cause\":\"unknown; a run-wide digitizer configuration, synchronization, or waveform-processing mismatch is more plausible than an isolated PMT or LVDS failure\",\"action\":\"First inspect raw waveforms and per-subrun feature distributions across several affected and unaffected channels. Verify digitizer thresholds, firmware/configuration, channel mapping, and feature-extraction settings against a known-good run; also check board matching, synchronization, queue occupancy, and digitizer-versus-trigger-board rates. If a configuration mismatch is confirmed, restore the validated configuration and start a new run. If synchronization or readout failure is confirmed instead, restart only the affected digitizer/DAQ component according to the operational procedure.\",\"confidence\":0.58,\"supporting_historical_runs\":[1605,1642,2126],\"reasoning_summary\":\"OBSERVED: anomalies affect all 129 subruns and 10,816 channel-entries, with several channels showing extreme nPulses and pulse-shape z-scores. The total trigger rate remains stable near 4.86, while LVDS has no persistent zero, missing, high, or low signal pins. INFERRED: the broad persistence with nominal trigger/LVDS behavior favors a global digitizer-side or analysis/configuration mismatch over a localized PMT-base or LVDS fault. Runs 1605, 1642, and 2126 partially resemble the extreme nPulses anomalies, but no exact historical match exists; 2126 additionally had missing channels and confirmed board-matching failure, which are not observed here. UNKNOWN: raw-waveform behavior, board matching, synchronization, queue occupancy, digitizer rates, and within-run configuration changes are unavailable and are required to distinguish configuration mismatch from digitizer/DAQ readout failure.\"}"
        }
